Z||000007||Motorhead||05-08-99||01:14 PM||||Where is all this NOS nonsense for the 2.5 coming from?<p>No offense to anyone, but this engine redlines at 4500 RPM.  The reason for this is because the internals are notoriously weak! Even the block is considered insufficiant for any serious performance mods. <p>I love my 4-banger Fiero, in fact I'm presently working on mods to improve its flow, some of which have been mentioned here, but if your considering NOS, you better have a couple of spare engines and be very profficiant at changing them.<br>
Z||000008||theogre||05-08-99||05:29 PM||||for comparison,,,,, the Mustang SVO and Turbo T-Bird use the 2.3l OHC motor built originaly for Ford Pinto and MustangII. Both cars were primarily targeted at fuel economy, and low sticker price. (Lee Iacoca's primary goal for Pinto was &lt;$2000 &lt;2000#. This goal is a direct cause of the fuel tank fire problem getting on the street. Ford knew the tank was bad before the first car was sold.)<p>The base designs of that motor are far stronger than the GM Cast Iron 4 cyl. ((This design is based on the 2.0l german made engine used thru the 72 model year)) It can just barly handle the turbo application. It was horrid for cooking off cams due to lack of oil at high revs.<p>It's another example of the problems of making an econo box motor act like a race motor. Ford has far more resources than all of us put together, and they still fried hundreds of cams.<p>There are things that can be safely done to a base Iron Duke motor but don't expect it to last under nitrous or similar use.
Z||000009||Lynx||05-09-99||01:09 AM||||Well of course nitrious would destroy the 2.5 ,but it would be fast "for a few days". I would never put nitrious in any vehicle its stupid and what it does to any motor is WAY expensive. As for the ? of removing the cat, Yes it will help by reduceing the back pressure on the exhaust. It dosent do a whole lot but it does some. Remeber it is illegal. Also if your looking at a new intake alway get the Holey TBI first.
Z||000010||Eric||05-09-99||10:43 AM||||You're right about the cat Lynx. oh, no pun intended.  Removal of the cat will free up maybe 1 to 2 horsepower at high RPM.  And most likely you will lose torque at lower revs.  I would like to see dyno results of before and after the cat was removed, but at this point I think my cat will stay on my car.<p>A built engine can handle nitrous just fine, Lynx.  Good quality hypereutectic or forged pisons, in the V6, can take over 300 horsepower.  Not sure about the 4 banger, sounds bad.
