Z||000015||Hock||05-25-99||11:32 AM||||with the right combination you can build an IRON DUKE to handle a modest 200-250hp on stock rods, crank, and block.  Hot Rod did a story a couple of years ago on the old Dukes.  the only weird thing about it was that the engine pumped out over 900hp.  no lie, find the issue.  i am currently working on an 82 Iron Duke for my 85 t-top.  it already has a Comp Cam 260H cam in it that is .489" lift.  the cylinder head will soon be recieving Speed Pro valves and Comp Cam valve springs.  the block will also be seeing Speed Pro forged pistons, polished and balanced stock rods and (hopefully) a balanced crank.  so far the block which has 20,000 miles on it has been bored .060 (nto recomended for daily driving) has held up just fine.  just like have learned and now live by when it comes to building engines "Research first to eliminate as many possible problems before they have a chance to happen."
Z||000016||Motorhead||05-25-99||02:23 PM||||Hock-- I know the issue of Hot Rod you're referring to, in fact when I read your post I just had to go dig itout![August 1986 issue]  The engine they built was based on the Super Duty engine. This is a completely different animal!<p>I've got the article in front of me as I write this.<p>Parts list includes Super Duty block, Pontiac p/n 10027633<br>aluminum cylinder head p/n 10038433,the list is quite lengthy.<p>This same artical lists aftermarket parts to upgrade the stock engine to 140 HP, but reccomended not exceeding this level because of weak internals and engine block!<p>The Rated Horsepower for the completed engine worked out at 205 @ 6500RPM.<p>Hope you got a big pocketbook, because it's either buy the Super Duty components, or new engines on a regular basis!<br> <p>
Z||000017||SCCA FIERO||05-25-99||05:26 PM||||The 4-cylinder Iron Duke/Tech IV engine that comes in your new Fiero or Firebird is not a performance engine, and its performance potential is severely limited by the strength of its internal components. The lightweight crankshaft becomes structurally unstable above 5000 rpm and may fail under repeated abuse. Fragile connecting rods and pistons designed for low-speed operation are also prone to failure at high engine speeds, and the stock cylinder block doesn't have the rigidity or strength to withstand substantial power increases. Nevertheless, some enthusiasts will no doubt want to upgrade the performance of their stock engines. You can approach our target of 180 hp with a normally aspirated, modified engine, but its durability at this power level is questionable. Hence, there are two basic methods of hopping up your Pontiac 4-cylinder: one involves the use of selected bolt-on parts to upgrade the stock engine to a more reasonable 140-hp range; the other is on the construction of a complete Super Duty engine that will put you over 200 hp. 
Z||000018||SCCA FIERO||05-25-99||05:33 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.users.uswest.net/~racing2win/Fiero_Fun.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.users.uswest.net/~racing2win/Fiero_Fun.htm</A> For those of you who don't have the 86 issue of HOT ROD
